  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#character-references\r\n\r\n> In certain cases described in other sections, text may be mixed with character references\\. These can be used to escape characters that couldn't otherwise legally be included in text\\.\r\n\r\nFollowing the logic, in those certain cases, whatever is not a character reference constitutes valid text (unless explicitly prohibited for other reasons). Now, in the definition of \"decimal character reference\", for example, there is no mention that a reference-like string with omitted semicolon still constitutes a \"character reference\"; thus it reads as if the string is valid text. Either text should be prohibited from containing those kinds of strings in all contexts that allow character references, or it should be stated that those strings are still \"character references\" (not text), but must not be used in conforming documents.",
